From d4d8abe009fe5b4696a63a5cb7b7d55a1208b4e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Lukschander <oliver.lukschander@golf.at>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 08:10:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm: apple: dptx: widen the tunnel set_hpd timeout to 8s as a
 diagnostic

Both 0128 boots (dcpext1, the correct pipeline) stall identically right
after request_display succeeds: dptxport_set_hpd() times out at its
default 1000ms, then release_display, then a retried validate, each in
turn -- three consecutive AFK timeouts on the same channel. DCP's own
firmware separately sends DEVICE_NOT_RESPONDING/DEVICE_NOT_STARTED and
DEACTIVATE a few seconds later, unprompted.

Whether that's a real internal AUX retry our 1s host timeout is cutting
off, or a genuinely failed handshake, was open. The strongest evidence
so far points to genuine failure: tb_dp_wait_dprx() (generic, non-Apple
thunderbolt/tunnel.c) polls the same DPRX_DONE hardware bit completely
independently of DCP/AFK, starting at tunnel-up with its own 12000ms
budget -- and it never asserts either, in both runs, a window already
far more patient than the 1s AFK timeout. But afk_service_call_timeout()
silently discards a reply that arrives after its caller's timeout with
no log trace, so today's captures cannot actually distinguish "DCP never
replies" from "DCP replies late and we already stopped listening."

dptxport_set_hpd_timeout() already exists and is the exact knob needed;
this changes the one call site in dcp_dptx_connect() to use it directly
with an 8s budget instead of the plain 1s wrapper -- long enough to
exceed DCP's own apparent ~5s internal give-up, short of the tunnel
layer's 12s budget. dcp->hpd_mutex is held for the extra duration, but
it is scoped to this DCP instance (315c00000.dcp) and not shared with
the panel's own (389c00000.dcp), so this cannot stall the internal
display. Single call site changed; no other behavior touched. Full
reasoning in notes/2026-09-24-0129-widen-set-hpd-timeout-diagnostic.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
index 6c24ef6..0fe8c5f 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
@@ -1445,7 +1445,19 @@ static int dcp_dptx_connect(struct apple_dcp *dcp, u32 port)
 	}
 	dcp->dptxport[port].connected = true;
 	if (dcp_is_typec_output(dcp)) {
-		ret = dptxport_set_hpd(dcp->dptxport[port].service, true);
+		/*
+		 * Diagnostic (candidate 0129, see notes/2026-09-24-0129-*.md):
+		 * every dcpext1 run so far stalls exactly here, and every
+		 * outbound AFK call after it, at the normal 1000ms budget --
+		 * while tb_dp_wait_dprx()'s independent poll (generic
+		 * thunderbolt/tunnel.c, started at tunnel-up, 12000ms budget,
+		 * unaffected by this call either way) also never sees DPRX
+		 * assert. Widening only this one call's timeout exposes
+		 * whether DCP replies late (a real internal retry we were
+		 * cutting off) or never at all.
+		 */
+		ret = dptxport_set_hpd_timeout(dcp->dptxport[port].service,
+					       true, 8000);
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(dcp->dev,
 				"dcp_dptx_connect: failed to assert Type-C HPD: %d\n",
-- 
2.55.0

